<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Muhammad Abduh</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1849">1849</a>–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad 'Abduh, Mohammed Abduh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Muhammad Abduh was an Egyptian Islamic scholar, judge and reformer, a central figure of the Nahda and of Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a>. A disciple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a> from his student years at al-Azhar, he argued that Muslims must use reason rather than simply rely on medieval interpretations of the texts. Exiled in 1882 for supporting the Urabi revolt, he published the journal al-'Urwa al-wuthqa with al-Afghani in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1884. After his return in 1888 he became a judge, and from 1899 until his death he was Grand Mufti of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/muhammad-abduh.jpg" alt="Muhammad Abduh" width="320" height="456" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Muhammad_Abduh.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Muhammad Abduh was born in 1849 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile-delta">Nile Delta</a>, to a father of Turkish ancestry and an Egyptian mother; his family belonged to the Ottoman Egyptian elite. He was educated at a private school in Tanta and at the Ahmadi mosque, and in these years studied under his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> uncle Darwish, a member of the reformist Madaniyya order, which stressed adherence to foundational teachings and shunned taqlid, the imitation of earlier authorities. In 1866 he entered al-Azhar in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, where he criticised the traditional curriculum and chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">Jamal al-Din al-Afghani</a> as his guide. Their relationship lasted eight years, and al-Afghani gave his mysticism a philosophical basis and drew him towards a rationalist reading of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Abduh received the degree of alim in 1877 and began teaching logic, theology and ethics at al-Azhar. In 1878 he became professor of history at the teachers' training college Dar al-Ulum and also taught Arabic. In 1880 he became editor-in-chief of the official gazette, the official gazette, which he used to spread anti-colonial ideas and calls for social and religious reform. British forces exiled him in 1882 for six years for supporting the Urabi revolt. He spent several years in Ottoman Lebanon, helping to establish an Islamic educational system, and in 1884 joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamal-al-din-al-afghani">al-Afghani</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> to publish al-Urwa al-Wuthqa.</p><p>After returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1888 he served as a judge in the Courts of First Instance of the Native Tribunals and became a consultative member of the Court of Appeal in 1891. As Grand Mufti from 1899, he was involved in decisions considered liberal, such as allowing meat butchered by non-Muslims and accepting loan interest. This endeared him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evelyn-baring-1st-earl-of-cromer">Lord Cromer</a> but caused a rift with the khedive Abbas Hilmi and the nationalist leader Mustafa Kamil Pasha. He proposed reforms to al-Azhar's examinations, curriculum and working conditions, and in 1900 founded the Society for the Revival of Arabic Literature. He died of renal cell carcinoma in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> on 11 July 1905.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Abduh wrote the Risalat al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tawhid">Tawhid</a>, a commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, and articles in al-Manar and al-Ahram. He argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> reproved slavish imitation of tradition and gave humans independence of will and thought, and he held that education, religious and scientific, was the best means of reform. He opposed polygamy where it led to injustice between wives and criticised superstition and the veneration of saints, while remaining sympathetic to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a>. He urged friendship among the schools of thought within Islam and between Muslims, Christians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. Opponents accused him of being an unbeliever; followers regarded him as a reviver of Islam.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Muhammad Abduh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jamal al-Din al-Afghani and <b>Muhammad Abduh</b> argued that Islam, rightly understood, was a religion of reason, compatible with modern science, and called on Muslims to return to the principles of their faith and to exercise independent judgement, ijtihad, instead of following later authorities without question.</blockquote><a href="/movement/nahda">Nahda · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the 19th century, as European colonial powers dismembered most of the Ottoman Empire, Jamal al-Din al-Afghani (1837–97), <b>Muhammad Abduh</b> (1849–1905) and Rashid Rida (1865–1935) preached Islamic alternatives to that decline.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/islamism">Islamism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Jamal al-Din al-Afghani, <b>Muhammad Abduh</b> and Rashid Rida were the major leaders of the modern movement, active in anti-colonial efforts against European penetration of Muslim lands, and they held Islamic unity to be the strongest force for mobilising Muslims against imperial domination.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/pan-islamism">Pan-Islamism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Shubra Khit, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/egypt">Egypt</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.03,30.71&amp;year=1885" aria-label="Shubra Khit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Muhammad Abduh"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5052 4912h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5052 4912h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.03,30.71&amp;year=1885">What was here in 1885? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
